    "description": "Mineral remote sensing uses measured spectral differences to map surface materials and geological indicators. Research in southeastern Iran demonstrates how satellite imagery can support copper exploration when interpreted alongside field samples and laboratory analysis.",
    "tags": ["Remote sensing", "Satellite imagery", "Mineralogy", "Copper exploration", "Kerman"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T12:19:45Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T12:19:45Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Mineral Remote Sensing in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore satellite-based mineral mapping in Iran, a Kerman copper case study, spectral libraries, field validation, and ASTER archive limitations."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "spectral-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the mineral signals at the surface",
        "content": "Spectroscopic remote sensing identifies materials through the way they interact with light at different wavelengths. The United States Geological Survey uses laboratory, field, airborne, and spacecraft spectrometers to investigate and map materials. Its spectral libraries include minerals, mixtures, and rock coatings, providing reference measurements against which observations can be interpreted. [s1]\n\nA mineral map therefore depends on more than a visually striking satellite image. Reference spectra help establish what a measured feature might represent, while mixtures and coatings explain why a surface may not behave like a pure laboratory mineral. This is particularly relevant when moving between a hand specimen, a field measurement, and an image covering a much larger area. These are related kinds of evidence, but they operate at different observational scales. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-case",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Copper exploration in the Kerman region",
        "content": "A study of the Zamin-Hossein area in southeastern Iran combined ASTER, Landsat-8, and Sentinel-2 imagery. Researchers mapped hydrothermal alteration—changes in rocks associated with hot fluids—and iron-rich weathering zones called gossans. They used ASTER shortwave-infrared information for alteration minerals and complementary imagery for iron-bearing surface materials. [s2]\n\nThe work paired image analysis with field samples, laboratory spectra, X-ray diffraction, and drill-core evidence. X-ray diffraction helps identify crystalline minerals, providing an independent check on interpretations based on reflected light. The researchers found that locally measured mineral spectra improved their alteration mapping relative to the standard-library comparisons used in the study. Their reported advantage for Sentinel-2 in iron-oxide mapping belongs to this particular investigation; it is not a universal ranking of satellites. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "archive",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why the image acquisition date matters",
        "content": "ASTER requires a specific historical caution. NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory states that its shortwave-infrared detectors stopped functioning properly and that data acquired since April 2008 are unusable because of saturation and severe striping. The advisory distinguishes this failure from the visible/near-infrared and thermal-infrared subsystems. It also identifies quality concerns affecting some earlier shortwave-infrared acquisitions. [s3]\n\nConsequently, a recent research publication can legitimately use much older ASTER imagery. The Iranian case study used an archived acquisition from August 2001. Publication date and image date answer different questions: one dates the analysis, while the other dates the observed surface. An account of contemporary alteration mapping should retain that distinction rather than imply that every input depicts present conditions. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "interpretation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From mineral indication to geological explanation",
        "content": "The broader value of remote sensing is its ability to locate and map mineral-related evidence across extensive areas. USGS identifies applications both to geological formations and to minerals in sediments and mine-waste accumulations. Exploration is therefore only one use; surface-material mapping can also inform the investigation of disturbed mining landscapes. [s4]\n\nFor a reader evaluating an Iranian mineral map, useful questions concern the material actually detected, the reference measurements used, and the independent checks available. A surface-mineral interpretation supports a geological investigation; its relevance to an underground ore body must be demonstrated with additional evidence. The Kerman study is instructive precisely because it connected image interpretation to samples and drill cores rather than presenting imagery alone as proof of a deposit. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
